var RES=[
 {slug:'what-a-business-website-needs',cat:'Guide',title:'What a business website actually needs',read:5,date:'November 12, 2026',excerpt:'A short list of what matters on day one, and what can safely wait until later.',
  body:'<p>Most small business websites fail in the same way: too much about the business, not enough about what the visitor came to find. Start with the basics and get them right.</p><h2>The essentials</h2><ul><li>A plain statement of what you do, who it is for and where you work, visible without scrolling.</li><li>A page for each main service, written for the person buying it.</li><li>Your phone number, email and location on every page.</li><li>A layout that works properly on a phone, because that is where many visitors will see it.</li><li>Real photos of your work, your premises or your people.</li><li>One obvious next step, such as calling, booking or sending an enquiry.</li></ul><h2>What can wait</h2><p>Animations, long company histories and extra pages nobody asked for. A tight, accurate site you can finish and maintain beats a sprawling one that never launches.</p>'},
 {slug:'choosing-a-website-platform',cat:'Guide',title:'Choosing a platform for your website',read:6,date:'November 5, 2026',excerpt:'Website builders, content management systems and custom builds, and how to decide between them.',
  body:'<p>There are three broad routes: website builders, content management systems and custom-built sites. None is always right. The best choice depends on what the site has to do.</p><h2>Website builders</h2><p>Quick to start and fine for simple sites. You trade flexibility and long-term control for convenience.</p><h2>Content management systems</h2><p>A good fit when several pages change often and you want to edit them yourself. They need updating and a little looking after.</p><h2>Custom builds</h2><p>Best when your site has to do something specific, such as bookings, accounts or integrations. More planning up front, and more control afterwards.</p><h2>Questions to ask</h2><ul><li>What must the site do on day one?</li><li>Who will update it, and how often?</li><li>What will it need to do in two years?</li></ul>'},
 {slug:'website-launch-checklist',cat:'Checklist',title:'A website launch checklist',read:4,date:'October 28, 2026',excerpt:'Ten things to check before any website goes live.',
  body:'<p>Run through this list before a site goes live. It takes about an hour and saves a lot of embarrassment.</p><ul><li>Every page proofread, with names, hours and contact details checked.</li><li>All forms tested, and the messages arrive where they should.</li><li>Links and buttons clicked, with no dead ends.</li><li>The site checked on a phone, a tablet and a desktop.</li><li>Page titles and descriptions written for every page.</li><li>Redirects set up from any old addresses.</li><li>SSL working, so the address starts with https.</li><li>Analytics installed and a sitemap submitted to search engines.</li><li>Backups running and a restore tested.</li><li>Your Google Business Profile pointing at the new site.</li></ul>'},
 {slug:'local-seo-basics-pei',cat:'Guide',title:'Local SEO basics for PEI businesses',read:5,date:'October 20, 2026',excerpt:'How to make it easy for people nearby, and visitors planning a trip, to find you.',
  body:'<p>Local search comes down to being easy for search engines, and for people, to understand: who you are, what you do and where.</p><h2>Start with your Google Business Profile</h2><p>Claim it, fill in every field, choose accurate categories, add photos and keep your hours current, especially through the seasonal changes common on the Island.</p><h2>Keep your details consistent</h2><p>Use the same business name, address and phone number on your site, your profile and any directories.</p><h2>Write for places and services</h2><p>A page for each main service, and honest mentions of the communities you serve, helps visitors and search engines alike.</p><h2>Ask for reviews</h2><p>Ask happy customers, and reply to every review, good or bad.</p>'},
 {slug:'prepare-for-a-design-project',cat:'Checklist',title:'What to prepare before a design project',read:4,date:'October 9, 2026',excerpt:'A short list that makes the first conversation faster and the result better.',
  body:'<p>You do not need everything below, but each item you bring makes the project smoother.</p><ul><li>Your goals: what should the website help you do?</li><li>Two or three websites you like, and what you like about them.</li><li>Your logo files, brand colours and fonts, if you have them.</li><li>Photos of your work, premises or people.</li><li>Any text you already have, even rough notes.</li><li>The name of the person who will approve decisions.</li><li>Access details for your domain and any current hosting.</li></ul><p>If you are missing some of these, that is fine. Part of the job is helping you work out what to gather.</p>'},
 {slug:'how-long-does-a-website-take',cat:'Notes',title:'How long does a website take?',read:3,date:'September 30, 2026',excerpt:'What really decides the timeline, and how to keep a project moving.',
  body:'<p>The honest answer is that it depends, but it depends on a small number of things.</p><h2>What shapes the timeline</h2><ul><li>Scope: how many pages and features there are.</li><li>Content: whether text and photos are ready or still to be written.</li><li>Feedback: how quickly decisions and approvals come back.</li><li>Integrations: whether the site connects to other tools.</li></ul><h2>Keeping it moving</h2><p>The most common cause of delay is waiting for content. Agree early who is writing what, and by when. Small, focused sites can come together quickly when content and decisions are ready.</p>'}
